  "at": "2026-11-11T16:15:24.046120661Z",
  "text": "OK, so our aim is to work through M-repl-circus completely, and this is scoped as \"multi-day\" though my guess is it will probably take us less than one day.  The first criterion that I think we need to look at is producing an actual \"legal\" cascade for each turn.  If we can produce even one, that would be a great start and I could put it into Mojira.  On that note I want to lean into the \"Mosaic\" referent over there so that we can display pictures, even though Mojira is  largely a text-based browser. Of course, we have nearly 40 years or improvements in what \"graphics\" means so we can make graphics that are clickable or whatever we want (including navigable/interactable via Conkeror style text hints!).  Anyway, I think we need to work out a graphical representation for a cascade.  \n./futon5a/essays/moran-1971-agent-cascades/reading-moran-1971.md is worth rereading and figure-1-cascade-of-64-patterns.png is, I think an example from the Moran paper that I found stylistically nice.  Of course, underneath that we'll have our category theory model.  But, basicaly, the acceptance condition for this criterion is to develop a *graphical* stepper.  On the one hand, each turn can be thought of as \"potentially\" a cascade (a null cascade if no patterns are found) and some kind of wiring diagram that would run through a session, probably showing how the individual turn cascades hook up into a bigger diagram \u2014 which could run vertically, a bit like the famous git merge diagrams, but here showing the flow across cascades, intents, etc.  We had some start on something like this in the Voxterm \"Diagram\" tab but it wasn't complete yet.  The relevance to the GCC is is that the local cascades would all be local regions inside the GCC."
 },
